    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 187,
      "text": "PR22/373\nIMF Executive Board Concludes 2022 Article IV Consultation\nwith Mexico\nFOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE\nWashington, DC – November 4, 2022: The Executive Board of the International Monetary\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 187,
      "end_char": 340,
      "text": "Fund (IMF) concluded the Article IV consultation1 with Mexico.\nAfter stagnating unexpectedly in the second half of 2021, the economic recovery picked up\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 340,
      "end_char": 529,
      "text": "pace in the first half of 2022 owing to catch-up momentum, improving labor market conditions,\nand fiscal policy support. Headline inflation rose from about 3 percent at the end-2020 to 7.4\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 529,
      "end_char": 712,
      "text": "percent by end-2021 and to 8.7 percent by August this year. Economic activity is expected to\nslow from 2.1 percent in 2022 to 1.2 percent in 2023, given weaker U.S. and global growth\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 712,
      "end_char": 932,
      "text": "and tighter global financial conditions. Inflation is projected to plateau in the second half of\n2022 and then gradually decline.\nThe government has maintained a broadly neutral fiscal stance in 2022, targeting a fiscal\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 932,
      "end_char": 1126,
      "text": "deficit of 3.8 percent of GDP. It has used largely untargeted subsidies to mitigate the rise in\nthe cost of living, offsetting the windfall gains from oil revenues. The gross debt of the public\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1126,
      "end_char": 1312,
      "text": "sector (by staff’s definition) is estimated at about 56 percent of GDP. With inflation above\ntarget, the central bank has increased the policy rate to 9.25 percent in a series of hikes.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1312,
      "end_char": 1496,
      "text": "Reflecting more resilient domestic demand growth compared to output, the current account\nposted a deficit of 0.4 percent of GDP in 2021 and is expected to widen to around 1 percent of\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1496,
      "end_char": 1664,
      "text": "GDP in 2022-2023. International reserves remain at a comfortable level.\nThe Mexican banking system remains well-capitalized, and its profitability continues to recover\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1664,
      "end_char": 1850,
      "text": "from the impact of the pandemic. Risk analysis as part of the Financial Sector Assessment\nProgram (FSAP), conducted in parallel with the Article IV consultation, found that the systemic\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1850,
      "end_char": 2044,
      "text": "vulnerabilities and system-wide liquidity risks in the financial system remain broadly contained,\ngiven high capital buffers, low private sector leverage, and no sign of stretched asset prices.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2044,
      "end_char": 2273,
      "text": "However, tail risks bear close monitoring. Financial sector policies have been strengthened in\nrecent years, but further steps are necessary to maintain the resilience of the financial system\nunder the evolving risk environment.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2273,
      "end_char": 2483,
      "text": "Executive Board Assessment2\nExecutive Directors agreed with the thrust of the staff appraisal. They welcomed the\nauthorities’ prudent macroeconomic policies in the face of a surge in inflation. They noted that\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2483,
      "end_char": 2668,
      "text": "economic growth is expected to slow in the near term reflecting weaker U.S. growth and\ntighter global financial conditions. With increased risks in a more turbulent global environment,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2668,
      "end_char": 2801,
      "text": "1 Under Article IV of the IMF's Articles of Agreement, the IMF holds bilateral discussions with members, usually every year. A staff\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2801,
      "end_char": 2936,
      "text": "team visits the country, collects economic and financial information, and discusses with officials the country's economic developments\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00017",
      "start_char": 2936,
      "end_char": 3067,
      "text": "and policies. On return to headquarters, the staff prepares a report, which forms the basis for discussion by the Executive Board.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00018",
      "start_char": 3067,
      "end_char": 3200,
      "text": "2 At the conclusion of the discussion, the Managing Director, as Chairman of the Board, summarizes the views of Executive Directors,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00019",
      "start_char": 3200,
      "end_char": 3389,
      "text": "and this summary is transmitted to the country's authorities. An explanation of any qualifiers used in summings up can be found here:\nhttp://www.IMF.org/external/np/sec/misc/qualifiers.htm."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
