    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 236,
      "text": "2\nDirectors agreed that Mexico is well placed to navigate the challenges, owing to its very strong\nmacroeconomic policies and policy frameworks.\nDirectors welcomed Banco de México’s proactive approach to addressing the inflation surge.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 236,
      "end_char": 424,
      "text": "With significant uncertainty about the inflation path in 2023 and upside risks, they concurred\nthat further increases in the policy rate and maintaining a restrictive policy stance may be\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 424,
      "end_char": 651,
      "text": "needed for some time. They also generally underscored the importance of clear policy\ncommunication. Directors recommended aligning wage increases more closely with expected\ninflation and the productivity of lower wage workers.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 651,
      "end_char": 837,
      "text": "Directors agreed that the envisaged neutral fiscal stance in 2022 and 2023 is appropriate.\nThey noted that retail fuel price smoothing, while reducing cost pressures, had a sizable cost\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 837,
      "end_char": 1023,
      "text": "on the budget, and generally encouraged the authorities to shift toward more targeted support\nshould oil prices rise again in order to protect other priority spending. Contingency plans\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 1023,
      "end_char": 1218,
      "text": "should be prepared for a swift policy response should downside risks materialize. Increasing\nfiscal buffers in the short term and reforming the institutional framework for greater flexibility in\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1218,
      "end_char": 1394,
      "text": "the longer term would be important. Directors noted that the floating exchange rate should\ncontinue to act as a shock absorber, although foreign exchange intervention could be\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1394,
      "end_char": 1553,
      "text": "considered if there is a significant worsening of market illiquidity.\nDirectors agreed that the financial system remains resilient post pandemic, welcomed the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1553,
      "end_char": 1731,
      "text": "favorable assessment of the effectiveness of Mexico’s financial stability framework, and\nbroadly supported the key policy recommendations of the 2022 Financial Sector Assessment\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1731,
      "end_char": 1916,
      "text": "Program. They concurred that additional measures, including an update of the financial sector\noversight and crisis management frameworks, are needed to keep up with a changing risk and\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1916,
      "end_char": 2082,
      "text": "regulatory landscape. After aligning the legal and regulatory AML/CFT framework with\nFinancial Action Task Force standards, the focus should now be to strengthen the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2082,
      "end_char": 2289,
      "text": "effectiveness of the AML/CFT regime, including through adequate resources, ensuring\navailability of high-quality beneficial ownership information, and monitoring emerging financial\nrisks related to fintech.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2289,
      "end_char": 2468,
      "text": "Directors urged the authorities to broaden the supply side policy agenda for higher and more\ninclusive growth. Efforts should include measures to address corruption and crime and\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2468,
      "end_char": 2646,
      "text": "strengthen the rule of law, increase human capital investment, tackle infrastructure\nbottlenecks, reduce labor and product market rigidities, and promote financial deepening and\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2646,
      "end_char": 2870,
      "text": "inclusion. Directors recommended a gradual increase in productive spending, financed by tax\nreforms, to improve human and physical capital.\nDirectors generally noted the need to take further steps toward reducing greenhouse\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2870,
      "end_char": 3108,
      "text": "emissions. Fostering greater private sector participation in the energy sector would help boost\ncompetitiveness and investment.\nIt is expected that the next Article IV consultation with Mexico will be held on the standard 12-\nmonth cycle."
    }
  ]
}

