{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 5",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 163,
      "text": "MEXICO\nSTAFF REPORT FOR THE 2022 ARTICLE IV CONSULTATION\nOctober 18, 2022\nKEY ISSUES\nContext. Mexico’s economy has recovered more gradually from the pandemic than\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 163,
      "end_char": 333,
      "text": "many peers. Even so, inflation has accelerated and has become more entrenched, as\nelsewhere. Domestic and external financial conditions tightened in the past year, while\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 333,
      "end_char": 559,
      "text": "near-term growth prospects for the U.S., Mexico’s main trading partner, have\nweakened. The risks of capital outflows have risen. These new challenges compound\nMexico’s long-standing problems of low growth and high inequality.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 559,
      "end_char": 721,
      "text": "Outlook and risks. Economic activity is expected to slow in the second half of 2022\nand in 2023, given weaker U.S. and global growth and tighter global financial\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 721,
      "end_char": 890,
      "text": "conditions. Inflation is projected to plateau in the second half of 2022 and then\ngradually decline. The balance of risks to the outlook is tilted to the downside, with\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 890,
      "end_char": 1074,
      "text": "higher domestic and global inflation, a sharper tightening of global financial conditions,\nand a sharper slowing in U.S. growth as the main downside risks.\nMain Policy Recommendations\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1074,
      "end_char": 1246,
      "text": "Fiscal policy. Moving towards more market-based fuel pricing would create room for\nproviding targeted income support to vulnerable households, help rebuild fiscal buffers,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1246,
      "end_char": 1482,
      "text": "and increase flexibility for fiscal policy to respond to downside risks. A permanent\nincrease in productive spending, financed by tax reform, would enhance growth while\nunderscoring Mexico’s commitment to a sustainable fiscal position.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1482,
      "end_char": 1708,
      "text": "Monetary policy. With near-term upside risks to inflation, Banco de México should raise\nthe policy rate further to a firmly restrictive level.\nFinancial sector policies. Policy priorities include strengthening the capacity of\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1708,
      "end_char": 1879,
      "text": "regulatory agencies, enabling supervision of financial groups on a consolidated basis,\nfurther enhancing supervisory techniques and methodologies, introducing supervision\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1879,
      "end_char": 2043,
      "text": "and regulation of cyber risks, and upgrading the macroprudential toolkit.\nStructural policies. Mexico should prioritize supply side policies to increase growth and\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2043,
      "end_char": 2223,
      "text": "job creation including by strengthening the rule of law and governance, increasing\nincentives for labor market formality, and promoting competition, including in the\nenergy sector."
    }
  ]
}
